save__mmfdb_artifact.row_grain
   _item.name                "_mmfdb_artifact.row_grain"
   _item.category_id         mmfdb_artifact
   _item_type.code           code
   _item.mandatory_code      no
   _mmfdb_schema.table_name  mmfdb_artifact
   _mmfdb_schema.column_name row_grain
   _item_description.description
;     What one row of a tabular artifact IS.
;
;     Two tables can only be joined once it is known what their rows count.  A
;     burst table and a dwell table are both "results of the same analysis" and
;     are not the same shape: a dwell subdivides a burst, so a dwell table is
;     finer, and a table of fused bursts is coarser than the bursts it fused.
;     Formats that cannot state this force every result onto one grid, and the
;     relations that do not fit that grid are then carried outside the format
;     or lost.  Stating the grain is what lets an artifact-to-artifact edge
;     (see _mmfdb_edge.source_row_column) be resolved to rows.
;
;     `spot` and `region` are not synonyms and the difference is not pedantry.
;     A spot is a point-like detection -- a localisation, with a position and
;     at most a width.  A region is an arbitrary connected set of pixels that
;     owns its own pixel list: a cell nucleus, a hand-drawn area, a watershed
;     basin.  A table of nuclei declared at `spot` grain is a false statement
;     about its rows, of the same kind as calling a file of binned traces a
;     photon stream, and it is false in the one field a reader consults to
;     decide what may be joined to it.
    loop_
    _item_enumeration.value
      photon
      burst
      dwell
      segment
      pixel
      voxel
      frame
      line
      molecule
      track
      spot
      region
      curve_point
      state
      species
      channel
      pair
      file
      histogram_bin

