save__mmfdb_edge.source_node_id
   _item.name                "_mmfdb_edge.source_node_id"
   _item.category_id         mmfdb_edge
   _item_type.code           code
   _item.mandatory_code      yes
   _mmfdb_schema.table_name  mmfdb_edge
   _mmfdb_schema.column_name source_node_id
   _item_description.description
;     What this edge points AT -- the artifact the derived one came from.
;
;     The column has existed in the hand-written DDL since the table was first
;     created; declaring it here is what lets a reader resolve it rather than
;     guess.  Leaving it undeclared had a concrete cost: a writer with nowhere
;     to put the parent reference put it in `relationship_type` instead, so
;     asking a file for the relationship between two artifacts returned an
;     integer where a term was expected -- and the *relation* itself, the thing
;     that says HOW the derived data relates to the primary, was never recorded
;     at all.
;
;     In a serialised container this is an object UID.  In the database it is
;     an artifact or operation id; `source_node_type` says which.
     save_

save__mmfdb_edge.target_node_id
   _item.name                "_mmfdb_edge.target_node_id"
   _item.category_id         mmfdb_edge
   _item_type.code           code
   _item.mandatory_code      yes
   _mmfdb_schema.table_name  mmfdb_edge
   _mmfdb_schema.column_name target_node_id
   _item_description.description
;     What this edge points FROM -- the derived artifact.
;
;     Implicit in a container, where an edge is a tag on the derived object and
;     the target is therefore the object carrying the tag.  Declared because the
;     database stores edges as free-standing rows, where it is not implicit.
     save_

save__mmfdb_edge.source_row_column
   _item.name                "_mmfdb_edge.source_row_column"
   _item.category_id         mmfdb_edge
   _item_type.code           line
   _item.mandatory_code      no
   _mmfdb_schema.table_name  mmfdb_edge
   _mmfdb_schema.column_name source_row_column
   _item_description.description
;     Column of the source table holding the key this edge joins on.
;
;     An edge between two artifacts says the tables are related.  It does not
;     say which rows correspond, and a great deal of information has been lost
;     historically by leaving that to positional alignment: two tables merged
;     by counting rows agree only while both have the same rows in the same
;     order, and disagree silently the moment one of them does not.  Naming the
;     key column makes the join declared data rather than a convention a reader
;     has to already know.
;

save__mmfdb_edge.target_row_column
   _item.name                "_mmfdb_edge.target_row_column"
   _item.category_id         mmfdb_edge
   _item_type.code           line
   _item.mandatory_code      no
   _mmfdb_schema.table_name  mmfdb_edge
   _mmfdb_schema.column_name target_row_column
   _item_description.description
;     Column of the target table holding the key this edge joins on.  See
;     _mmfdb_edge.source_row_column.
;

